# How to cite DiresQ.
#
# GitHub reads this and puts a "Cite this repository" button on the repo page.
# Zenodo reads it when it archives a release and uses it to fill in the record.
#
# BEFORE THE FIRST ZENODO RELEASE, two things must be real:
#   * every author's legal name (a bibliography entry reading "Londopy &
#     Skythe7" will not be accepted anywhere)
#   * the doi field below, which can only be filled in AFTER Zenodo mints one,
#     because a DOI cannot be reserved ahead of a GitHub release
#
# Validate with: pip install cffconvert && cffconvert --validate

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: If you use this software, please cite it as below.
type: software

title: "DiresQ: tracking the responders, not just the disaster"

abstract: >-
  A disaster response tracker built on the observation that existing tools map
  incidents while leaving the volunteers who self-deploy into them unlogged.
  DiresQ records who went where, holds each responder to a check-in interval,
  and escalates automatically when somebody stops answering: after a bounded
  silence the server files a report at their last known position. Reports are
  ordered so that an incident nobody has committed to outranks one that is
  merely understaffed, and staffing state is resolved by taking the most
  cautious signal from responders on scene rather than a majority. Includes a
  START triage helper, an ICS-214 activity log export, an offline check-in
  queue with idempotent retries, and a signed low-bandwidth packet format
  intended for LoRa transport.

authors:
  # TODO: replace with legal names before any academic submission.
  # Handles are kept in `alias` so both forms survive.
  - given-names: FIRSTNAME
    family-names: LASTNAME
    alias: Londopy
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"
  - given-names: FIRSTNAME
    family-names: LASTNAME
    alias: Skythe7
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"

repository-code: "https://github.com/Skythe7/DiresQ"
url: "https://skythe7.github.io/DiresQ"

license: Apache-2.0
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-08-03

keywords:
  - disaster response
  - emergency management
  - volunteer coordination
  - accountability
  - crisis informatics
  - START triage
  - ICS-214
  - LoRa
  - offline first

# Filled in after the first Zenodo release. Use the *concept* DOI (the one
# that always points at the newest version), not a version-specific one,
# unless you are citing a specific release on purpose.
# doi: "10.5281/zenodo.XXXXXXX"
